21. International Monetary System: Bretton Woods and the Present System
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: IV | Topic: International monetary system – Bretton Woods System, operation of the present system
Introduction
The international monetary system is the set of rules and institutions governing exchange rates, payments and reserves. It has moved from the gold standard to Bretton Woods to the present managed float.
The gold standard (1870-1914)
Currencies were fixed in terms of gold. Adjustment was by the price-specie-flow mechanism. It gave stability but forced domestic deflation to maintain parity, and collapsed during World War I and the Depression.
The Bretton Woods System (1944-1971)
Created at the Bretton Woods Conference, with Keynes and White as principal architects. Features:
• Adjustable-peg: currencies pegged to the US dollar, with a 1 per cent band; the dollar was convertible into gold at $35 an ounce.
• IMF to provide short-term balance of payments loans and oversee rules; World Bank (IBRD) for reconstruction and development.
• Devaluation permitted only in cases of fundamental disequilibrium.
• Capital controls were allowed.
Breakdown: the Triffin dilemma. The world needed dollars for liquidity, requiring US deficits, which eroded confidence in the dollar's gold convertibility. US inflation from Vietnam spending and speculation led Nixon to suspend gold convertibility in August 1971. The Smithsonian Agreement (1971) failed, and by 1973 major currencies floated.
The present system (post-1973)
The Jamaica Agreement (1976) legalised floating and demonetised gold. It is a mixed system:
• Major currencies (dollar, euro, yen, pound) float; central banks intervene in dirty floats.
• Some countries peg to a currency or basket; others use currency boards or unions (the euro).
• SDRs serve as a reserve asset, and the IMF provides surveillance and lending.
• Capital flows are largely liberalised.
Merits and problems
Merits: monetary independence, automatic adjustment, and smooth absorption of shocks. Problems: exchange rate volatility and misalignments, currency crises (Mexico 1994, East Asia 1997), global imbalances, dollar dominance and reserve accumulation by emerging economies.
Conclusion
The present non-system provides flexibility but lacks coordination, leading to proposals for reform of global reserves, IMF governance and capital flow management.